  {
    "episode_id": 45242,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45242.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 41,
    "turn_text": "Probably a little bit of both. Look, I believe in the old saying, God closes one door, but opens another. And I had to go through that door. You have to walk through that door or roll through that door in my case. And someone had suggested that I think about a run for political office. And I thought about it. It was around the time of the first, the Constitutional Convention back, I think, was around in 1985. And I thought about it. I thought it was a good way to be involved with the community, to get back to the community that's given to us and my family and I. And so I ran. And I fortunate to be elected. It was only a one-year position. I've said many times, I found, not only did I find something where I felt I was giving back, but I really found something that I enjoyed. And one thing led to the next, and a couple years later, I ran for the state legislature, and I was elected there, and then in 94, just serving as six years in the House, in the legislature, I ran for Secretary of State, and was elected there, and then served there for six years, and then in 2000, ran for Congress. And that's what I've been doing ever since, what I thought started off as a way of giving back. I didn't know it was going to be a lifelong journey in public life, but here we are.",
