  {
    "episode_id": 45242,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45242.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 73,
    "turn_text": "So many things to be proud of, and I've been able to serve the State of Rhode Island. I'm very proud of the work we've done in cyber security, of course, in national security. I'm a senior member of the House Armed Services Committee, and I'm also on the senior member of the Homeland Security Committee. And it came across cyber as a major threat to the country, both government and private sector, especially critical infrastructure. And we did a deep dive, and we worked hard to better protect the country in cyberspace. And most proud of it were the legislation I passed to create the National Cyber Director and the Executive Office of the President. So that's a whole office that I was able to create on with my colleagues. But then also the work I've done in disabilities, something that's very important, trying to bring down barriers and open up doors for other people with disabilities to provide opportunities for people. One of the bills we passed, of course, is the legislation to create a passenger with disabilities bill of rights. so that when people with disabilities are traveling, that they have a right to be treated properly with respect and have their mobility devices cared for. So we're working hard to make other things available for people with disabilities when they travel, and I have an Air Carry Access Amendments Act to accomplish that. Also, the work that we've done on career and technical education, that's another area I'm very proud of because we want to make sure that our young people are getting the right skills, they need to do jobs that are available right now in every manufacturer, for example. There are jobs available that go unfilled because people don't have the right skills to do those jobs. So I co-chair the Career and Technical Education Caucus in Congress. That's a bipartisan group. I chair it with G.T. Thompson, my Republican colleague from Pennsylvania. And he and I work hard to make sure that the funding is there for Career and Technical Education as well as the program. So we actually passed the Career and Technical Education for the 21st Century Act, which is a revamp of the Perkins program. And we updated the program to make sure this cultural alignment between what the skills that businesses need and what's being taught in the classroom. So I'm very proud of the work on the Virginia class submarines and other areas of national security. We build the best nuclear submarines right here on Rhode Island and they're a vital component of our national security.",
